## Findings and resolution

| Original issue | Why it matters | Revision |
|---|---|---|
| Normal template already names scenario, location and assertion | Good foundation; not enough to prove delivered behavior | Preserve all three; add actual delivery and layer/assertion assessment |
| Self-satisfaction initially described by "primary deliverable" | Mixed behavior changes could be hidden by test-oriented framing despite a later caveat | Whole-scope test; material behavior changes require normal coverage |
| Pure-hardening exclusion and self-satisfaction overlap | A check altering admission may itself change material behavior | Distinguish pure test additions from changed enforcement semantics |
| Exclusions can be mistaken for document-wide keywords | Titles, negation or historical examples could bypass material coverage | Classify each material component; exclusions must describe the whole actual scope |
| Validation runner instructed to auto-insert stubs before strict checking | Sealed/admitted plan contents could be changed at execution time | Explicit draft helpers only; all sealed-plan validation is read-only |
| Lint described as formal CI gate without caller-level integration guarantees | Existence of a checker is not admission across all supported routes | Require mapped provisioning, amendment, plan-validation and bound-dispatch checks |
| Marker communicates a human applicability decision | An unchecked marker is not semantic proof | Require inspectable rationale and resolution of material contradictions |
| Original checker accepts brief self-satisfying prose and keyword-based N/A reasons | Structure alone cannot establish valid applicability | Require concrete supporting evidence and semantic review; no claim heuristics solve it |
| Original normal-form checker recognizes test-looking paths | A path can exist while assertions miss the recurrence | Identify case, affected boundary and detecting assertion; verify actual delivery |
| N/A and accepted deferral exist but can be merely claimed | "Race," unavailable infrastructure or "(accepted)" is not sufficient evidence | Preserve permitted reasons; inspect alternatives and verify actual follow-up commitment |
| Accepted follow-up format assumes numeric references/status wording | Nonportable references can create needless blockers | Use actual stable host references and committed lifecycle, preserving real approval |
| Announcement required before the first heading | Can conflict with supported provisioning order | Announce before publication; candidate preparation follows host input order |
| Current lint can scan archives under current syntax | Updating policy could retroactively reject sealed historical contracts | Revision-aware enforcement; preserve historical plans and records |
| Original text treated selected tier as a ceiling never to change | Inadequate coverage might be rationalized under an insufficient tier | Preserve binding; permit only supported authorized tier amendments when needed |
| No tracked requirement-to-delivery verification contract | A valid plan or task Done label could be mistaken for actual coverage | Required revision-bound handoff enabling optional separate read-only verification |

The checker observations apply to the inspected project snapshot, not all apps.
This package specifies necessary future integration behavior; it does not claim
that the inspected checker, provisioner or bound runner already implements it.

## Mapping the requested additions

| Requested outcome | Governing location |
|---|---|
| Concrete recurrence, affected-layer test, detecting assertion before publication; actual delivery or valid exception | SKILL sections 2 and 5 |
| Normal, permitted N/A and genuine self-satisfaction through actual creation/amendment routes; correct exclusions | SKILL sections 1–3; enforcement acceptance cases |
| Mixed material work cannot escape through titles, negation or unrelated exclusions | SKILL section 1; enforcement classification and negative cases |
| Invalid current declarations rejected across provisioning, amendment, validation and supported bound execution before workloads; no sealed edits | SKILL section 3; enforcement ordering and sentinel cases |
| Historical contracts/IDs/bindings/ledgers/workloads preserved; honest cooperative boundary | SKILL sections 4 and 6; enforcement migration boundaries |
| Tracked requirement-to-delivery handoff enabling separate read-only assessment | SKILL section 5; delivery-handoff reference; review optional unless selected/required |

## Owner-directed clarification

The first delivered revision could be read as requiring a separate verifier for
every delivery and renewed review after every repair. The owner approved removing
that unintended prerequisite. The required handoff and executor coverage assessment
remain; separate review is optional unless explicitly selected by the owner or
called for by an identifiable already-approved requirement. Unselected review is
reported as "not selected," not a blocker or pending required acceptance.

Historical preservation means keeping sealed records and their applicable contracts
intact. It does not make retired skills competing instructions or require archive
reconstruction/old receipts before current maintenance or unrelated new work.

## Pre-implementation and post-Start clarification

Both skills now require automatic pre-implementation preflight, not merely plan
publication or pre-test checking. They distinguish automatic preparation and
authorized supported amendment from read-only admission. Active native Plans can
be updated where supported, but do not replace local seals or bindings. Mid-task
gaps require pausing affected work and preserving evidence before amendment.
Start is not blanket approval, retrospective authorization or proof of a platform
hook. Already adequate plans and already-covered authorization are reused.

## Review scope

The text was reviewed against the requested outcomes in three passes:

1. Classification: exclusions, mixed work, material changes to guard code,
   negation, test-oriented framing, valid N/A and deferral.
2. Lifecycle: candidate preparation, publication, failed amendments, sealed-plan
   immutability, pre-dispatch denial, historical revision preservation and tiers.
3. Evidence: planned versus delivered files, assertion adequacy, residual risk,
   requirement mapping, separate read-only verification and platform boundaries.

These are authoring reviews, not independent verification or executable proof of
an application's enforcement. The enforcement reference supplies positive and
negative acceptance cases for an app implementation; those cases have not been
run by writing this package.

## Package checks

Before delivery, check frontmatter and line/description limits, local reference
links, coverage of the requested contract points, ZIP root layout, archive
integrity, and byte-for-byte equality of archived files to canonical sources.
Report the observed package-check outcome separately in chat.

No tier PASS, app installation, historical migration, live validation, independent
verifier result or Replit platform-completion control is asserted.
